  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1035 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Locally heavy rainfall is possible overnight for \nthe SE 1/2 of the area, but the severe threat has ended.\n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates a weakening cold front bisecting\nthe CWA, and slowly dropping farther to the SE. As the sfc \nfront becomes nearly parallel to the flow aloft, it may stall \novernight near or just south of the VA-NC border. Latest SPC \nmesoanalysis shows that while there is still decent ML CAPE \nacross southern VA and NE NC (> 1000 J/Kg), sfc-based CAPE has \ndiminished significantly for most of the region outside of \nportions of NE NC (primarily due to the stronger storms from \nearlier today). Therefore, any severe threat overnight is quite \nlow, and even the heavy rain threat has been scaled back. With \nthat said, still could see some locally heavy rainfall along and\nsouth of the frontal boundary through around 4 AM, and PoPs \nremain likely for the SE portion of the FA. Lows overnight will\nrange from the mid 60s NW to the low-mid 70s in the far SE.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorms later Friday into Friday night\nwill be capable of producing heavy rainfall, potentially \nleading to localized Flash Flooding.\n\nThe front lingers over the region tomorrow before pushing \ntoward the coast on Saturday, keeping our area downstream of the\nslow-moving trough axis with rounds of convection persisting \nFriday and Saturday along coastal areas. As mentioned earlier \nmodels continue to remain in slight disagreement on where the \nfrontal boundary stalls on Friday. With the disagreement a Flood\nwatch has not been issued at this time. However, a Flood watch \nmaybe needed for late Friday into Saturday once location of the \nstalled boundary is noted. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures Sunday \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nModel ensembles remain in good agreement that the main upper trough\naxis shifts offshore by Sunday, driving the cold front south and\neast of the region. In its wake, surface high pressure builds over\nthe Mid-Atlantic, introducing a drier air mass and seasonable\ntemperatures through early next week.",
